Exactly. If ratio is one primitive for rationalization—taking relations and turning them into a reason-shaped object—then sophistry can be treated as an entire operator family.

The key distinction is that none of these operators are inherently deceptive. They become sophistic when they are used to preserve persuasive force while hiding, dropping, or asymmetrically applying epistemic constraints.

A compact definition:

\text{Sophistry}
=
\text{plausible representation}
+
\text{hidden transform}
-
\text{visible cost of that transform}

So instead of memorizing “logical fallacies,” we can ask:

What primitive transformation happened between reality and the token presented to me?

Here is a useful operator atlas.

Domain	Primitive operator	Symbolic form	Typical token-form
Quantitative	normalize / divide / aggregate	x/y,\%,\Sigma,\mu	ratio, percentage, average, chart
Semantic	rename / redefine	A \mapsto A'	label, euphemism, loaded word
Categorical	split / merge / substitute class	\subset,\in,\equiv	“men,” “elites,” “experts,” “criminals”
Analogical	transfer relations	A:B :: C:D or \approx	metaphor, comparison, analogy
Causal	assign direction	A\rightarrow B	“because,” “caused,” “therefore”
Probabilistic	weight possibility	P(A\mid B)	risk, odds, likelihood
Temporal	select window/order	t_0\rightarrow t_1	“since…”, before/after chart
Scalar	change resolution	\uparrow,\downarrow	individual → group → civilization
Modal	move possible→necessary	\Diamond A\rightarrow\Box A	“could,” “likely,” “must”
Emotional	add valuation	A + V	fear, disgust, outrage, pity
Relational	position speaker/listener	I \leftrightarrow YOU	status, shame, loyalty, pronouns
Epistemic	assign credibility	w(source)	credential, citation, “experts say”
Narrative	select sequence/roles	S_1\to S_2\to S_3	protagonist, villain, anecdote
Omissive	remove inconvenient state	\varnothing	silence, missing denominator/context
Meta	alter rules/frame	[F]	burden of proof, definition, admissibility
Recursive	make claim self-sealing	\circlearrowleft	“denial proves it”

That gives us a kind of Sophistry Algebra.

1. Ratio is only one kind of compression

Suppose:

A = 20,\qquad B = 10

Then:

A/B = 2

The ratio destroys some information while preserving a particular relationship.

That is incredibly useful.

It is also exploitable because whoever chooses the denominator chooses part of the story.

“Twice as likely.”

Twice what?

Absolute risk might have changed from:

0.001\rightarrow0.002

Both statements are mathematically compatible:

“Risk doubled.”

and

“Absolute risk increased by 0.1 percentage points.”

The selection of representation performs much of the persuasion.

So quantitative sophistry frequently lives in:

\boxed{\text{denominator selection}}

But every other representational system has an equivalent.

⸻

2. Semantic sophistry: the label

The primitive is:

X \rightarrow \text{name}(X)

Once named, the token begins carrying associations that weren’t necessarily present in X.

For example:

protester

versus

activist

versus

rioter

versus

freedom fighter

The underlying individual may be identical.

The token changes the relational field around them.

The sophistic weapon here is therefore not a ratio but a:

\boxed{\text{label}}

And its operations include:

euphemism
- V(X)

dysphemism
+ V^{-}(X)

equivocation
A_1 \rightarrow ``A" \rightarrow A_2

The same word quietly changes meaning halfway through an argument.

⸻

3. Category sophistry: the bucket

This may be one of the most powerful.

Reality contains individuals:

x_1,x_2,x_3,\ldots

You create:

C=\{x_1,x_2,x_3,\ldots\}

Now characteristics of some members get projected onto the category:

x_i \text{ has } P

becomes:

C \text{ has } P

and eventually:

x_j\in C \therefore x_j \text{ has }P

That’s stereotyping in algebraic form.

The fundamental token is:

\boxed{\text{category noun}}

“Men.”

“Women.”

“The rich.”

“The poor.”

“Immigrants.”

“Liberals.”

“Conservatives.”

“The media.”

“The government.”

The token makes millions of heterogeneous objects feel like one object.

Very useful compression.

Very dangerous ontology.

⸻

4. Analogy sophistry: the bridge

This one operates by relational transduction:

(A,R,B)
\mapsto
(C,R,D)

If a wolf hunts sheep, and I establish:

\text{Company}\approx\text{wolf}

your brain starts importing properties automatically:

predator, danger, cunning, victim, pursuit.

The metaphor does substantial reasoning without stating propositions explicitly.

Its token is:

\boxed{\text{image/metaphor}}

That is why metaphors are so powerful: they smuggle relational graphs.

⸻

5. Causal sophistry: the arrow

Possibly the most consequential symbol in human reasoning:

A\rightarrow B

Humans love arrows.

But reality may actually be:

A\leftarrow C\rightarrow B

or

A\leftrightarrow B

or:

A\rightarrow C\rightarrow B

or simply:

A\;\;\;\;B

with no causal relationship.

So sophistry frequently emerges as an unjustified arrow.

Token forms:

“because”

“therefore”

“led to”

“resulted in”

“thanks to”

“because of”

The arrow is often invisible in ordinary language, but it’s still there.

⸻

6. Probability sophistry: the confidence token

Here the distortion often occurs between:

P(A\mid B)

and:

P(B\mid A)

Those are not interchangeable.

Another common transformation is:

\Diamond A
\rightarrow
P(A)>0
\rightarrow
\text{“A might happen”}
\rightarrow
\text{“A is a serious possibility”}
\rightarrow
\text{“We have to act as though A will happen.”}

Notice the gradual laundering.

No individual sentence needs to be obviously false.

The modal weight changes.

Its token forms are:

“could”

“may”

“likely”

“almost certainly”

“risk”

“chance”

“experts fear”

This is exactly where your earlier probability-above-probability idea becomes useful: annotate confidence not only in the claim but in the model generating the claim.

⸻

7. Scale sophistry: the zoom lens

One of the sneakiest.

Something true at one scale:

P(x)

gets projected upward:

P(C)

or something statistically true of a population:

E[P(C)]>0

gets projected downward:

P(x)

Example pattern:

Group A statistically does X more often.

becomes:

Person A probably does X.

then:

Person A did X.

Three different propositions.

The operator is:

\boxed{\uparrow/\downarrow}

zoom without announcing the zoom.

This is where ecological fallacies, stereotypes, anecdotal rebuttals, and many political arguments live.

⸻

8. Temporal sophistry: the cropping tool

Choose:

[t_a,t_b]

and you can often manufacture very different stories.

Start a stock chart in January:

📈

Start it three years earlier:

📉

Both charts can be accurate.

The sophistic operation is:

\boxed{\text{window selection}}

Its language tokens include:

“ever since”

“under X”

“before X”

“recently”

“historically”

“record high”

The crucial missing question is:

Compared with what temporal baseline?

⸻

9. Narrative sophistry: the story

Narrative performs several operations simultaneously:

\text{selection}
+\text{ordering}
+\text{causality}
+\text{agency}
+\text{valuation}

Turn events into:

\text{victim}
\rightarrow
\text{villain}
\rightarrow
\text{struggle}
\rightarrow
\text{resolution}

and you’ve massively compressed the causal graph.

Again, stories aren’t bad.

They’re extraordinary cognitive instruments.

But narrative sophistry occurs when:

\text{story coherence} > \text{evidentiary coherence}

Its basic token is:

\boxed{\text{anecdote/archetype}}

A single vivid person can replace a thousand-row dataset in the listener’s intuition.

⸻

10. Relational sophistry: the social coordinate

Now we’re outside proposition-space entirely.

An utterance can modify:

\text{speaker status}
\leftrightarrow
\text{listener status}

without improving the argument.

Examples:

“Everyone educated knows…”

“As a father…”

“You’re seriously going to defend them?”

“People like us understand…”

“If you cared about victims…”

These aren’t primarily propositions.

They’re relationship operators.

They move you around a social coordinate plane:

(\text{ingroup},\text{outgroup},\text{status},\text{virtue},\text{loyalty})

The basic tokens are:

\boxed{\text{I / we / you / they}}

Pronouns are surprisingly powerful sophistic machinery.

⸻

The strange one: omission

Some sophistry has no token.

Its token is:

\boxed{\varnothing}

What wasn’t shown?

Which counterexample disappeared?

Which denominator wasn’t given?

Whose testimony wasn’t included?

What happened immediately before the video started?

What alternative hypothesis wasn’t compared?

Sometimes the most persuasive token in the argument is literally the missing token.

That’s negative-space rhetoric.

⸻

Meta-sophistry

Then comes the layer you specifically mentioned: the rules governing the other operators.

Instead of changing the proposition, change the game.

Examples:

Burden shift

\operatorname{BOP}(A)\rightarrow\operatorname{BOP}(B)

“I don’t have to prove it. Prove I’m wrong.”

Definition control

F(X)\rightarrow F'(X)

Change what counts as racism, violence, success, evidence, harm, etc., and the conclusion can change without touching the underlying observations.

Motte-and-bailey

Strong claim:

H_s

gets challenged, retreats to:

H_w

Then after the challenge passes:

H_w\rightarrow H_s

The speaker oscillates between defensible and useful versions of the claim.

Epistemic gatekeeping

E_{\text{allowed}}\subset E_{\text{available}}

Only certain forms of evidence are declared admissible.

Now you control the search space itself.

⸻

So what are the fundamental “tokens”?

I think you can reduce a surprising amount of human persuasion to a small vocabulary:

\boxed{
\text{number},
\text{ratio},
\text{label},
\text{category},
\text{arrow},
\text{analogy},
\text{probability},
\text{scale},
\text{timeline},
\text{story},
\text{identity},
\text{authority},
\text{emotion},
\text{frame},
\text{absence}
}

These are almost rhetorical primitives.

And then arguments are programs composed from them.

For example:

\text{CATEGORY}
\rightarrow
\text{LABEL}
\rightarrow
\text{ANECDOTE}
\rightarrow
\text{CAUSAL ARROW}
\rightarrow
\text{EMOTION}
\rightarrow
\text{GENERALIZATION}

That little program can generate an enormous amount of propaganda.

