The deeper Pantheon move

Don’t ask merely:

“What fallacy is this?”

Ask:

What representation operator was applied?

What information did it preserve?

What information did it destroy?

What did that destruction make easier to believe?

So every token gets a delta shadow:

T = \text{presented token}

\Delta_T =
\text{source reality}
-
\text{information preserved by }T

And the most important sophistic information often lives inside:

\boxed{\Delta_T}

The ratio’s shadow is the denominator and raw magnitude.

The category’s shadow is within-group variance.

The label’s shadow is alternate descriptions.

The arrow’s shadow is confounders and reverse causality.

The story’s shadow is omitted events.

The analogy’s shadow is where the mapping breaks.

The authority’s shadow is the underlying evidence.

The emotional token’s shadow is what you’d believe with the affect removed.

The frame’s shadow is all the questions the frame prevented you from asking.

So the generalized antidote to sophistry isn’t merely “logic.”

It is representation inversion:

\boxed{
\text{token}
\rightarrow
\text{infer operator}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstruct discarded dimensions}
\rightarrow
\text{compare alternative encodings}
\rightarrow
\text{see what survives}
}

That’s considerably more powerful than a fallacy dictionary because it works on rhetoric, statistics, narratives, interfaces, politics, advertising, scientific communication, interpersonal arguments—and even your own internal reasoning.